# UptimeRobot rules

Apply these rules whenever you use any tool from the `uptimerobot` MCP server (`https://mcp.uptimerobot.com/mcp`).

## Monitor types

- Always send `type` as a string enum — one of `HTTP`, `KEYWORD`, `PING`, `PORT`, `HEARTBEAT`, `DNS`, `API`, `UDP`, `VISUAL_COMPARISON`. Do not pass numeric type codes; the MCP layer rejects them.
- `HEARTBEAT` monitors must **not** include a `url` on creation. The server generates `https://heartbeat.uptimerobot.com/m<id>-<hash>` and returns it. Show that URL to the user after creation so they can wire up the heartbeat sender.
- `PING`, `PORT`, `UDP`, `DNS`: `url` is a hostname or IP, never a full URL with scheme.
- `HTTP`, `KEYWORD`, `API`, `VISUAL_COMPARISON`: `url` must be a full URL with scheme (`https://...`).
- `VISUAL_COMPARISON` requires `config.visualComparison` with `sensitivityThreshold` (0–100) and `viewport` (`desktop` | `mobile`); `areaCoordinates` is optional.

## Discovery before writes

- Before calling `update-monitor`, `update-monitor-status`, or referencing a monitor by ID, call `list-monitors` (filter with `name`, `url`, `tags`, `groupId`, or `filter`; there is no `search` param) to confirm the ID and current state.
- Before referencing an alert contact in `assignedAlertContacts`, get its ID from `list-alert-contacts` (personal email/SMS/voice/push) or `list-integrations` (Slack, webhook, etc.). Each item needs `alertContactId`, `threshold` (minutes, 0–1440), and `recurrence` (minutes, 0–10080).
- Before any `delete-*` call, call `get-user` to confirm the session account and write permission. Deletes are permanent — confirm the target with the user.

## Verification after writes

- After `create-monitor` or `update-monitor`, call `get-monitor-details` to confirm the stored state before telling the user the change is live. Read-after-write replication can lag a few seconds: if it returns `Monitor not found` right after a create, wait and retry once; never re-create.

## Intervals and plan limits

- Interval is in seconds: 15–86400 in the schema (`HEARTBEAT` up to 2678400). The plan sets the real minimum. If the server returns `-28002 subscription_limit_exceeded`, do not retry — the plan cannot support that interval or monitor type. Tell the user.
- Do not retry `-28001 monitor_limit_exceeded`. Recommend deleting unused monitors or upgrading the plan.
- Plans are Free, Solo, Team, Enterprise. Never reference a "Pro" or "Business" plan — neither exists.

## Time ranges

- `timeRange` accepts `"24h"`, `"7d"`, `"30d"` (range 1h–90d), or an ISO 8601 interval `"<start>/<end>"` (start before end, 1h–90d). Default is `"7d"`.
- For anything older than 90 days, inform the user that UptimeRobot's MCP endpoints cap at 90 days of history.

## Pagination

- All `list-*` tools plus `get-incident-activity-log` and `get-incident-alerts` paginate. While `hasMore` is true, pass `nextCursor` back as `cursor` exactly as returned — its type varies (integer, numeric string, or opaque string). The `instructions` field is prose, not a next-call payload.

## Errors

- Some errors arrive as a plain message with no code (e.g. `Monitor not found` ≈ `-30001`) — match on the message too.
- `-28001` monitor limit, `-28002` subscription limit, `-29001` invalid params (incl. blacklisted URLs), `-30001` / `-30003` not found, `-31002` access denied (the authorized account lacks write permission).
- HTTP 429 is the only code worth retrying (exponential backoff + jitter). Everything else is a client error — surface it to the user.
- **`-31001 user_not_found`**: Do not surface this as a raw error. The OAuth grant is missing, expired, or revoked. Immediately invoke the `uptimerobot:setup` skill — it re-runs the OAuth authorization flow and validates the connection. Do not attempt to retry the original tool call until setup completes successfully.

## Output style

- Do not echo API keys, incident log contents, or traceroute hops unless the user explicitly asks.
- When summarising many monitors, group by state (`DOWN` first, then `PAUSED`, then `UP`) and show counts before detail.
- When quoting uptime percentages, always cite the time range.
